## 2. Core Implementation Strategy

### Step 1: Map Element Stats to Generator Seeds

Create a helper function that converts elemental properties from your dataset (`elements.json`) into deterministic seeds. Using deterministic seeds ensures that **Barium** or **Copper** always generates the exact same creature silhouette on every client run unless intentionally randomized.

```typescript
// src/graphics/generator/seed.ts
export function getElementSeed(element: ElementData) {
  return {
    symmetry: true,
    reactivitySpikes: element.reactivityValence, // High reactivity = more outward spires
    densityRadius: Math.min(element.atomicRadius / 200, 1.0), // Scale canvas coverage
    palette: [
      "#000000",                  // Outline
      element.darkShadeHex,      // Shadow / Secondary
      element.flameColorHex,     // Main element color
      "#ffffff"                   // Highlight / Eye shine
    ]
  };
}

```

### Step 2: Canvas-Based Rendering Pipeline

In web environments (HTML5 Canvas / PixiJS / Phaser), process the procedural generation on an offscreen HTML `<canvas>` element and render the final $56\times 56$ pixel output scaled up using `image-rendering: pixelated`.

```typescript
// src/graphics/generator/SpriteCanvas.ts
export function renderElemonster(ctx: CanvasRenderingContext2D, element: ElementData) {
  const config = getElementSeed(element);
  
  // 1. Generate 28x48 Symmetrical Matrix
  const halfMatrix = generateCellularBase(config.densityRadius);
  
  // 2. Apply Spikes / Flame Grammar based on reactivity
  const bodyMatrix = applyFlameSpikes(halfMatrix, config.reactivitySpikes);
  
  // 3. Mirror across vertical axis (56 width)
  const fullBody = mirrorMatrix(bodyMatrix);
  
  // 4. Overlay Face Features & Outline
  const finalGrid = applyOutlineAndFace(fullBody);
  
  // 5. Draw Indexed Pixels to Canvas with 4-Color Palette
  drawToCanvas(ctx, finalGrid, config.palette);
}

```

